---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, essayUrl, howUrl, labUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay for the two idempotency principles, keys on commands and keys at Stripe (docs/domain.md,
// "The principle map" and "Idempotency"; Q33 · Q-principles). No lab page shows them, so the essay
// explains them itself and links the lab's index. Static, no island. Every claim about Stripe's
// or Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the date in READ, and every
// number from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS['idempotency-keys'];
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header/',
    title: 'The Idempotency-Key HTTP Header Field',
    by: 'an Internet-Draft of the IETF’s HTTPAPI working group, version 08 of 2026-10-22',
    for: '400 for a missing key, 422 for a key reused with another payload, 409 for a repeat while the first is still running, and an expiry policy each server publishes',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests',
    title: 'Idempotent requests',
    by: 'Stripe’s API reference',
    for: 'the status and body of the first request saved and returned to every retry, 500s included; keys that may be pruned once 24 hours old; parameters compared and a mismatch refused',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks',
    title: 'Receive Stripe events in your webhook endpoint',
    by: 'Stripe’s documentation',
    for: 'the same event delivered more than once, guarded by logging event ids; no promise of order; sandbox retries three times over a few hours, live mode for up to three days; a five-minute tolerance on the signature’s timestamp',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/sqlite-storage-api/',
    title: 'SQLite-backed Durable Object Storage',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'transactionSync() rolls back when its callback throws, and takes only synchronous storage operations',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/IdempotentReceiver.html',
    title: 'Idempotent Receiver',
    by: 'Gregor Hohpe and Bobby Woolf, Enterprise Integration Patterns (2003)',
    for: 'a receiver that can safely receive the same message several times',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'The lab',
    by: 'ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, none of them on idempotency keys; the Queue Lab’s workers check a message’s id before printing',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery makes a repeated request harmless: an Idempotency-Key stored in the same transaction as the command's events, a replay that returns the first answer, and the same idea again at Stripe and its webhooks."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A request that may have arrived is sent again, and the bakery has to tell a retry from a new
      order. This is how a key does that, why it only works when it is written with the order, and
      how the same idea meets Stripe, which keeps its own clock.
    </p>

    <h2>Place order, pressed twice</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor on a train orders three morning buns for a 09:15 pickup and presses Place order. A
      tunnel takes the signal, and the page says the API is unreachable. The order may never have
      arrived, or it may be placed and holding three buns while the answer was lost on the way back.
      The visitor presses again.
    </p>
    <p>
      A server that runs every request it receives places a second order, and the day’s count of
      morning buns drops by six. Only the server can tell the two apart, and only if they carry
      something that says they are one intent.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The usual first answer is a key the client makes and a table of keys the server has seen,
      checked before the work and written after it. That works until the process stops between the
      two. If the order commits and the key is lost, the retry finds no key and places the order
      again. If the key is written first and the order then fails, the retry finds the key and
      answers for an order that does not exist. A key kept in another store, a cache or a second
      database, has the same gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Replying “already done” to a seen key breaks the client too: the retry needs what the first
      attempt returned, the order’s id, its total and its hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      No lab page shows this one yet. The{' '}
      <a href={labUrl('Queue Lab')} rel="noopener">
        Queue Lab
      </a>{' '}
      shows the receiving half, workers that check a message’s id before printing, and{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>{' '}
      is where the family of techniques is explained.
    </p>

    <h2>The key is written with the order</h2>
    <p>
      Every POST to the API needs an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header holding a ULID. A missing
      or malformed one is refused with 400 <code>idempotency-key</code> before anything else runs (
      <code>workers/api/src/http/mod.rs</code>), the status the IETF’s draft gives for a missing
      key. The storefront makes the key once per attempt (<code>apps/web/src/lib/attempt.ts</code>):
      the same key while the cart and the form are unchanged and the last failure was the network or
      a 5xx, which may have been applied, and a new one after a 4xx, which applied nothing, or after
      any change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Placing an order passes the key through three objects, each of which remembers it. The Sandbox
      maps it to one order id in its <code>order_keys</code> table, so a retry addresses the same
      Order object. The day’s Schedule holds the places under <code>hold:</code> and the key. The
      Order records it last. The Schedule and the Order each keep a<code>commands_seen</code> table
      of the key, a SHA-256 hash of the request body, the answer they gave and the time (
      <code>workers/api/src/objects/stream.rs</code>), as every aggregate class does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The nuance is where that row is written. The Order appends its events, copies them to its
      outbox, sets its expiry timer and inserts the <code>commands_seen</code> row inside one
      <code>transactionSync</code>, which Cloudflare’s documentation says is rolled back if its
      callback throws. There is no moment when the order exists and its key does not, or the other
      way round. A key is only as good as the transaction it is stored in.
    </p>

    <h2>A replay is the first answer</h2>
    <p>
      When a command arrives, the object reads its row for the key before it decides anything (
      <code>check_command</code> in <code>crates/domain/src/outbox.rs</code>). No row: the command
      is decided and committed. A row with the same body hash: the stored answer goes back as it
      was, with <code>Idempotent-Replayed: true</code>, and 200 where the first answer was 201.
      Nothing is decided again and no event is appended, so the buns are counted once.
    </p>
    <p>
      A row with a different hash means the client sent one key with two bodies, which is the
      client’s bug, not a retry. It is refused with 422 <code>idempotency-key</code>, reason{' '}
      <code>key-reused</code>, the status the IETF draft gives for that case. Guessing which body
      was meant would place an order nobody asked for.
    </p>
    <p>
      A replay can be out of date. A hold released by its timer, or given back after the Order
      refused, would replay a stale “held”. Since the hardening of 2026-11-06 the day decides such a
      key again, as <code>Retake</code>: the same place is held again with the retry’s own 15
      minutes when there is room, or the retry is refused with 409 <code>hold-lost</code>, naming
      the next free slot.
    </p>

    <h2>The second edge: Stripe’s keys and Stripe’s clock</h2>
    <p>
      Stripe is a second system with its own state, so the same problem returns at the edge. The
      Billing object commits its decision first, then calls Stripe with a key the committed state
      names: <code>&lt;sandbox&gt;:&lt;ref&gt;:&lt;purpose&gt;:&lt;attempt&gt;</code> to create an
      intent, the purpose suffixed with the call for the others, such as <code>-refund</code> (
      <code>crates/app/src/stripe/request.rs</code>). The order’s id is the reference. The sandbox
      leads because two sandboxes born from one seed share their seeded ids, and Stripe’s reference,
      read on {READ}, says it saves the first status and body for a key, 500s included, keeps the
      key for at least 24 hours, and refuses a retry whose parameters differ. Without the sandbox, a
      second visitor’s payment would get the first one’s answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The order is paid only when Stripe’s signed webhook says so. Stripe’s documentation says an
      event can arrive more than once and in no promised order, and tells you to log the event ids
      you have processed. Billing records each id inside the event it produced, and its state keeps
      them as <code>webhooks_seen</code>, so a redelivery is answered 200 and changes nothing (
      <code>crates/domain/src/billing.rs</code>). Order is handled by refusing what cannot apply
      yet: a refund that arrives before its payment is answered 409, and Stripe delivers it again
      later. A payment that succeeds after its hold expired is recorded anyway, because the money
      moved, and the Order refunds it as late. It is the header’s idea with someone else’s clock: a
      webhook that comes twice or late changes nothing it should not.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A row per key per object.</strong> A placement writes three: the Sandbox’s mapping,
        and a <code>commands_seen</code> row in the Schedule and in the Order. Nothing prunes them:
        they live until the sandbox is wiped at 10:00 UTC, or until an object’s 36-hour backstop
        deletes it (<code>docs/domain.md</code>, “Idempotency”). The draft asks each server to
        publish its expiry, and this is the bakery’s: under a day and a half.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A 422 a client can earn.</strong> A client that reuses a key for a changed cart gets
        422, not an order. The storefront avoids it by minting a key for every changed attempt.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A body hash on every command</strong>, and the answer stored beside it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One key for every distinct call to Stripe</strong>, which is why the keys carry
        suffixes and an attempt number: Stripe refuses one key on two different requests.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in, issue a token in <a href="/app/">the console</a>, and open{' '}
      <a href="/checkout/">the checkout</a>. The order form’s Under the hood note shows its request
      as a curl with an <code>Idempotency-Key</code>. Run it once and the answer is 201 with a new
      order; run it again unchanged and the answer is 200, the same order, with
      <code>Idempotent-Replayed: true</code>. Change a quantity and keep the key, and the answer is
      422. Then pay for an order with the test card 4242 4242 4242 4242, and find the payment once
      in <a href="/app/">the console’s</a> ledger.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('idempotency-keys')}>idempotency keys</a> and{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('payment-keys')}>idempotency keys on payments and webhooks</a> on How it’s
      built, to the decisions that chose them,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0004')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0004
      </a>{' '}
      and{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0007')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0007
      </a>
      , or to <a href={essayUrl('raft')}>why a single writer needs no consensus</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
